A single scan is enough here. For each matching node, connect its previous node to its next node, then fix the next node's `prev` pointer. The head needs special handling because several matching nodes may appear at the beginning.

The list is changed in place. For example, with `4 <-> 2 <-> 2 <-> 7` and target `2`, the links become `4 <-> 7`.

```cpp
/*
Definition of doubly linked list:
struct ListNode
{
    int val;
    ListNode *next;
    ListNode *prev;
    ListNode()
    {
        val = 0;
        next = NULL;
        prev = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = NULL;
        prev = NULL;
    }
    ListNode(int data1, ListNode *next1, ListNode *prev1)
    {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
        prev = prev1;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode * deleteAllOccurrences(ListNode* head, int target) {
        while (head != NULL && head->val == target) {
            head = head->next;
            if (head != NULL) {
                head->prev = NULL;
            }
        }

        ListNode* current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            if (current->val == target) {
                ListNode* previous = current->prev;
                ListNode* nextNode = current->next;

                previous->next = nextNode;
                if (nextNode != NULL) {
                    nextNode->prev = previous;
                }
                current = nextNode;
            } else {
                current = current->next;
            }
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is checked once.

Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Instead of unlinking nodes from the old chain, this version creates a new DLL from values that do not match the target. It is easy to check because every added node is attached at the end of the new list.

Dry run: for `2 <-> 6 <-> 2` and target `2`, the first value is skipped, `6` becomes the new head, and the last value is skipped. The returned list contains only `6`.

This does not change the input nodes. The trade-off is that it uses new nodes and extra memory.

```csharp
/*
Definition of doubly linked list:
public class ListNode {
    public int val;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode prev;

    public ListNode() {
        val = 0;
        next = null;
        prev = null;
    }

    public ListNode(int data1) {
        val = data1;
        next = null;
        prev = null;
    }

    public ListNode(int data1, ListNode next1, ListNode prev1) {
        val = data1;
        next = next1;
        prev = prev1;
    }
}
*/

public class Solution {
    public ListNode DeleteAllOccurrences(ListNode head, int target) {
        ListNode newHead = null;
        ListNode tail = null;

        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.val != target) {
                ListNode newNode = new ListNode(current.val);

                if (newHead == null) {
                    newHead = newNode;
                    tail = newNode;
                } else {
                    tail.next = newNode;
                    newNode.prev = tail;
                    tail = newNode;
                }
            }
            current = current.next;
        }

        return newHead;
    }
}
```

The old list is scanned in `O(n)` time. The new list can contain up to `n` nodes, so the extra space is `O(n)`.

A small dummy node makes deletion easier because every real node then has a previous node, even when it is the original head. After removing all matches, the dummy is not returned, and the new head gets `prev = null`.

This also handles an empty list and a list where every value equals `target`.

