class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteAllOccurrences(ListNode head, int target) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        if (head != null) {
            head.prev = dummy;
        }

        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.val == target) {
                ListNode previous = current.prev;
                ListNode nextNode = current.next;

                previous.next = nextNode;
                if (nextNode != null) {
                    nextNode.prev = previous;
                }
                current = nextNode;
            } else {
                current = current.next;
            }
        }

        head = dummy.next;
        if (head != null) {
            head.prev = null;
        }
        return head;
    }
}
```

The traversal takes `O(n)` time. The dummy node is the only extra storage, so the extra space is `O(1)`.

This version separates the two jobs. The first loop moves the head past matching nodes. The second loop handles nodes that have a previous node, so the head case does not need a special branch inside that loop.

The links are updated directly, and no new list nodes are created. If the target is not found, the original list is returned unchanged.

