## Intuition
To remove all instances of a target value without managing complex node deletion logic in place, we can construct a filtered version of the list from scratch. By traversing the original list, we can simply ignore nodes that match the target and dynamically link the valid nodes together, treating the problem as a selective rebuilding process.

## Approach
We use a sentinel (dummy) node to anchor our new list and a curr pointer to build it. As we traverse the original list, whenever we find a node that doesn't match the target, we link it to curr and wire up the bidirectional pointers (.next and .prev). Once the traversal finishes, we clear any trailing pointers at the tail by setting curr.next = null, detach the new head's .prev pointer from the dummy node, and return the modified list.

## Solution
```javascript[]
/*Definition of doubly linked list:
class ListNode {
    constructor(val = 0, next = null, prev = null) {
        this.val = val;
        this.next = next;
        this.prev = prev;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    deleteAllOccurrences(head, target) {
        var result = new ListNode(-1, null, null);
        var curr = result;

        while (head !== null) {
            if (head.val !== target) {
                curr.next = head;
                head.prev = curr;
                curr = curr.next;
            }
            head = head.next;
        }

        curr.next = null;
        if (result.next !== null) {
            result.next.prev = null;
        }

        return result.next;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n) where n is the number of nodes in the linked list, as the loop traverses the entire list exactly once.

**Space Complexity:**
O(1) as we only use a constant amount of extra pointer space regardless of the input list size, excluding the original input nodes.
